 "capsule": "The earliest datable Balinese polity appears in the Belanjong pillar inscription of 914 CE from Sanur, written in Old Balinese and Sanskrit and naming a ruler, Sri Kesari Warmadewa, associated with the Warmadewa dynasty that dominated the island for the next four centuries. Under kings such as Udayana Warmadewa (r. c. 989-1011) and his queen Mahendradatta, sister of the Javanese king Dharmawangsa, Bali absorbed strong Javanese Hindu-Buddhist influence while retaining a distinct courtly and subak irrigation culture attested through royal inscriptions and temple foundations rather than a single fixed capital. This early kingdom's independence ended in 1343 when the Majapahit general Gajah Mada conquered Bali, installing the Kepakisan dynasty as vassal rulers.",
